<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics — A comprehensive analysis of over 20,000 Lichess chess games exploring win rates, opening strategies, rating dynamics, and game outcome patterns.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>20,058</strong> games analyzed</div>
      <div class="meta-item">Ratings <strong>784 – 2,723</strong> Elo</div>
      <div class="meta-item"><strong>80.6%</strong> rated games</div>
      <div class="meta-item">Source: <strong>Lichess.org API</strong></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="container">

<nav class="toc">
  <h2>Contents</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#overview">Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</a></li>
    <li><a href="#color-advantage">White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</a></li>
    <li><a href="#rating-analysis">Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</a></li>
    <li><a href="#opening-strategy">Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Success Rates</a></li>
    <li><a href="#time-control">Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</a></li>
    <li><a href="#key-findings">Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 1 ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2 class="section-title">1. Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</h2>
  <p>The dataset comprises <strong>20,058 chess games</strong> collected from the Lichess platform. Of these, <strong>16,155 (80.6%)</strong> are rated games affecting player Elo, while <strong>3,903 (19.4%)</strong> are casual. Player ratings span from <strong>784</strong> to <strong>2,723 Elo</strong>, representing a broad cross-section of online chess skill levels.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">20,058</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent2">
      <div class="kpi-value">10,001</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">White Wins</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">49.9% of all games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card red">
      <div class="kpi-value">9,107</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Black Wins</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">45.4% of all games</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">950</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Draws</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">4.7% of all games</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Game Outcome Distribution</h3>
  <p>The majority of games end in <strong>resignation (55.6%)</strong>, followed by <strong>checkmate (31.5%)</strong>. Timeout and draws each account for a smaller share. The relatively high checkmate rate (compared to professional chess) reflects the amateur-to-intermediate skill level of most players in the dataset.</p>

  <div class="chart-grid">
    <div class="chart-container">
      <h4>Winner Distribution</h4>
      <div class="chart-wrapper"><canvas id="winnerPie"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <h4>Victory Status Breakdown</h4>
      <div class="chart-wrapper"><canvas id="victoryPie"></canvas></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <h4>Victory Status: White vs. Black Breakdown</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="max-height:320px"><canvas id="vsColorBar"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Observation:</strong> White wins more often than black across checkmates (52.9% vs 47.1%) and resignations (52.4% vs 47.6%). Interestingly, in <strong>timeout</strong> games, white's advantage disappears — black actually wins slightly more timeout games (49.0% vs 48.4%), suggesting the first-move advantage is primarily a positional one that doesn't translate to time pressure.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 2 ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="color-advantage">
  <h2 class="section-title">2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
  <p>The perennial chess question: does going first matter? In this dataset, <strong>white wins 52.34% of all decisive games</strong> (excluding draws). While this is a meaningful edge, it's more modest than the ~55% often cited at grandmaster level — likely because at amateur ratings, tactical errors dominate over positional nuances.</p>

  <div class="kpi-row">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">52.34%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">White Win Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Of decisive games (excl. draws)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card accent2">
      <div class="kpi-value">47.66%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Black Win Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Of decisive games (excl. draws)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card amber">
      <div class="kpi-value">+4.68 pp</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">White Advantage</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Percentage point edge</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card purple">
      <div class="kpi-value">4.74%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Draw Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">950 of 20,058 games</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>White Advantage by Average Rating Bracket</h3>
  <p>The first-move advantage narrows as player skill increases. At sub-1200 Elo, white wins 52.7% of decisive games; at 2000+, this shrinks to <strong>50.6%</strong> — nearly coin-flip territory. Stronger players equalize better with the black pieces.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <h4>White Win Rate (Decisive Games) by Rating Bracket</h4>
