      <strong>White's first-move advantage is real but modest: 52.34% in decisive games (+4.68 pp).</strong> This edge narrows to just 50.6% at 2000+ Elo and inverts entirely in Bullet time controls (45.7% white WR). The advantage is most pronounced in Classical games (53.4%) and among 1200–1399 rated players (53.8%).
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-rating">Rating</span>
      <strong>Rating differential is the dominant predictor of outcome.</strong> A 200-point gap gives the favorite ~64% win probability; at 500+ points, the favorite wins 84.8%. Coaches and matchmakers should treat every 100-point gap as approximately a 7–8 percentage point shift in expected outcome.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-rating">Rating</span>
      <strong>Higher-rated players play longer games.</strong> Average game length rises 36% from sub-1200 (50.0 turns) to 2000+ (68.1 turns). This reflects deeper calculation, more complex positions, and fewer early blunders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-opening">Opening</span>
      <strong>The Sicilian Defense is black's best weapon.</strong> In 2,573 games (the most popular opening), black wins 52.0% of decisive games — one of only four openings where black outperforms white. Its asymmetric, combative nature gives black genuine winning chances.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-opening">Opening</span>
      <strong>The Bishop's Opening and English Opening are white's best-performing popular openings</strong> at 60.8% and 57.2% white WR respectively. The Ruy Lopez (55.7%) and Zukertort Opening (58.1%) also give white above-average results.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-opening">Opening</span>
      <strong>Avoid the Van't Kruijs Opening (1.e3).</strong> With only a 35.8% white win rate across 368 games, this passive opening concedes all of white's first-move advantage and then some. It has the worst performance of any opening in the top 15.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-opening">Opening</span>
      <strong>Medium-depth opening theory (4–12 ply) maximizes white's edge.</strong> White scores 53–55% in these ranges vs 50% or below in shallow (1–3 ply) or very deep (13+) openings. Investing in opening preparation up to about 6 moves deep yields the highest returns for white.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="tag tag-time">Time</span>
      <strong>55.6% of games end by resignation; only 31.5% by checkmate.</strong> The draw rate is just 4.7%, far lower than professional chess (~30–50%), reflecting the amateur-level play where decisive mistakes are common.
    </li>
    <li>
